    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 2 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Our Classroom News</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> I Will Write the Article About Our School Trip.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reading: reading_1: Write responses to questions about informational texts.<br>reading_2: Create written reflections on stories read in class.<br>phonemic_awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reading: Can respond to questions in a text about newsletter topics (e.g., 'When will this be?' 'What is your article about?' 'When did this happen?').<br>Can write reflections for the newsletter on short stories read in class (e.g., 'The story was funny.').<br>Can demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words (e.g., recognizing /ɑ/ in 'art' and /oʊ/ in 'show').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f0f0f0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Warm-up:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher displays pictures of the classroom's previous school trip to the Emberá Community and a sample "Classroom News" headline like "Our Exciting Trip!". Students quickly read the headline and share one word about what they remember. (e.g., "fun," "river," "art").</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Modeling:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reads aloud a short paragraph from a simple example article about a school trip. The teacher models the reading, emphasizing new vocabulary (e.g., 'article', 'newsletter', 'pictures', 'event') and clear pronunciation, particularly focusing on identifying short and long vowel sounds in single-syllable words (e.g., /ɪ/ in 'trip', /ɑ/ in 'art', /oʊ/ in 'show'). The teacher points to words as they are read.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this text about our school trip or a class presentation?" (Checks gist)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the article need pictures?" (Checks detail and 'need' for materials)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Classroom News".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Material:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">A short informational text titled "Our Class Trip to Emberá Community" designed as an article for "Our Classroom News." The text is 3-4 paragraphs long, uses vocabulary like 'article', 'newsletter', 'pictures', 'event', 'teacher', 'student', 'fun', 'exciting', 'write', 'report', and incorporates grammar structures: Present simple (e.g., "We need pictures for the classroom newsletter."), Future in affirmative (e.g., "I will write the article."), and Past simple (e.g., "Yesterday, we went on a field trip."). The text also includes potential topics for discussion, such as roles in creating a newsletter.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Competence:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Collaborate in writing projects, such as creating a classroom newsletter, by clarifying roles, responsibilities, and deadlines, and using appropriate language for teamwork (e.g., 'I will be responsible for the layout of the page.' 'Can you write the article about our school trip?' 'Let's decide who will collect the pictures.').</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Read and Answer):</b> Students read the article and answer: "What is this article mainly about?" and "Who is writing the article?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Read and Mark):</b> Students read statements and mark them T (True) or F (False) based on the article. (e.g., "The class went to the zoo yesterday." "They need photos for the newsletter.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Read and Match):</b> Students match sentence beginnings with endings from the text. (e.g., "We need..." matches with "...pictures for the newsletter.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reading comprehension of informational texts, responding to questions about past, present, and future events, and identifying short and long vowel sounds. Grammar focus: Present simple with questions, Future in affirmative and interrogative sentences, Past simple in affirmative and interrogative sentences. Vocabulary: 'article', 'newsletter', 'pictures', 'event', 'write', 'report', 'exciting', 'fun'.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap-fill (Reading & Completing):</b> Students reread the "Our Class Trip to Emberá Community" article. The teacher provides a worksheet with the article, but with key target vocabulary words (e.g., 'article', 'newsletter', 'pictures', 'write', 'went') and verb forms (past/future/present) missing. Students fill in the blanks using a provided word bank, focusing on recognizing words in context and grammatical accuracy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Question and Answer (Reading & Responding):</b> Students read a series of questions related to the article (e.g., "When did the class go on the trip?", "What will they write about?", "What do they need for the newsletter?"). They write short, complete sentence answers based on the text, using the past, present, and future tenses accurately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check & Phonemic Awareness (Reading & Identifying):</b> In pairs, students take turns reading specific sentences from the article aloud. As one student reads, the partner listens carefully and identifies two words containing short vowel sounds (e.g., /ɪ/ in 'trip', /ɛ/ in 'event') and two words with long vowel sounds (e.g., /aɪ/ in 'write', /oʊ/ in 'show'). They briefly discuss the sound. Then, each student writes a simple one-sentence reflection about the story they just read (e.g., "The story was exciting."). They read their reflection to their partner.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Classroom News".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Task Format:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a new, short, simple story (e.g., about a school event, a class project, or a local community activity, similar in length and complexity to the main article). The stories are varied to ensure different content. Their task is to collaboratively read their story and then prepare two items for "Our Classroom News":</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Story Reflection:</b> A short written reflection (2-3 sentences) about the story, expressing their opinion or a key takeaway, suitable for a newsletter column (e.g., "This story about our class art project was very creative.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Newsletter Questions:</b> Three open-ended questions based on specific facts from their story, which readers of the newsletter could answer (e.g., if the story was about an art project, "When did the art presentation happen?", "What materials did the students use?", "Who organized the event?"). They must use past, present, or future simple questions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">They will read their story together, identify key facts, discuss ideas for their reflection, and formulate their questions, clarifying roles and responsibilities within the group (e.g., "I will write the reflection paragraph," "Can you write the questions?").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Formative Assessment Strategy:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher observation checklist focusing on group collaboration, reading comprehension, and the quality of their written output. Teacher circulates and notes participation and language use.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Assessment Task:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Groups submit their written story reflection and their three generated questions about the story. The teacher will also briefly listen to students orally identify vowel sounds during the practice activities.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to questions about informational texts (demonstrated by the quality of generated questions based on facts).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the written reflection (1-2 sentences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'story', 'event', 'exciting') and grammar (present/past/future simple in questions/reflections).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds (observed during pair reading).</li>
                </ul>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I understand and write about short stories for our classroom newsletter?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was I able to find the important facts in the story I read?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting fact you read in today's stories for the newsletter?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did you face when creating questions about the text, and how did you solve them?"</li>
                </ul>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The activities effectively engaged students in reading informational texts and responding to questions. Most students were able to extract key facts and formulate simple reflections. The phonemic awareness focus helped reinforce sound-symbol correspondence in context. Some students needed additional prompting to correctly use past simple in interrogative forms. Adjustments needed: Provide more scaffolded examples of question formation for different tenses for the next reading task. Ensure a quick choral review of short and long vowel sounds with examples from the text at the start of the practice phase.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Connection to Next Lesson:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In Lesson 3, we will use the reflections and questions we created today to practice speaking and interacting with classmates about "Our Classroom News," asking and answering questions about the different articles.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>